Deferred. Do not start this without the trigger below.

Project fields are single-line key: value, so they cannot hold a set, and
there is no equivalent of bd's label filtering. A comma-separated field
covers the simple case badly: it invites parsing inside a value, which is
the thing the format has otherwise avoided everywhere.

Trigger: wanting to filter across a dimension that is not already a field.
Until then, adding a field is cheaper and reads better.

28 changes the calculation slightly — once values are validated by a
pattern, a comma-separated set has a grammar that check could enforce
rather than a convention it cannot see. That makes the bad option less bad,
not good.


Evidence from 38, 2026-09-18. The downstream project used beads for sixteen months and its
labels amount to one distinct value — feedback-2026-07-22, a dated batch
marker on 33 issues from a single afternoon's triage. No taxonomy ever
formed, and nothing was ever filtered across one. The trigger stands and has
not fired. Keep deferred.

A concrete want, 2026-09-21. This was deferred for lack of one.

An agent workflow the owner uses routes a ticket to an agent by domain
label, down a fixed priority list. It is the only routing input there is. A pidly project therefore
enumerates and dispatches but cannot route, because there is nothing on a
ticket for Route to read.

That workflow works around it for now: its pidly reader takes a project
field named "labels", whitespace-separated, if the project declares one. That
is a convention living in the consumer rather than in the format, which is the
shape this ticket exists to decide whether to fix.

Note what it does NOT settle. A field holding "engineering urgent" is a set
only by agreement — nothing validates it, `pidly field labels` prints the
string, and two projects will separate them differently. The question here was
never whether a set can be faked in a string; it is whether the format should
know about sets. This is a caller that wants one, not an answer.

See 6 for the workflow this came from.
